      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to use the concept of independent events in probability. Two events are independent if the occurrence of one does not affect the probability of the other. The probability of both independent events occurring is the product of their individual probabilities.\n\nGiven:\n- The probability it will rain on Saturday, \\( P(S) = 60\\% = 0.60 \\)\n- The probability it will rain on Sunday, \\( P(U) = 25\\% = 0.25 \\)\n\nWe need to find the probability that it will rain on both days, \\( P(S \\cap U) \\). Since the events are independent, the probability of both events happening is calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\nP(S \\cap U) = P(S) \\times P(U) = 0.60 \\times 0.25\n\\]\n\nNow, we perform the multiplication:\n\n\\[\n0.60 \\times 0.25 = 0.15\n\\]\n\nTo express this probability as a percent, we multiply by 100:\n\n\\[\n0.15 \\times 100 = 15\\%\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that it will rain on both days is \\(\\boxed{15}\\).",
