    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the probability that it will rain on both Saturday and Sunday, we need to use the rule of product for independent events. This rule states that the probability of two independent events both occurring is the product of their individual probabilities.\n\nGiven:\n- The probability it will rain on Saturday, \\(P(\\text{Rain on Saturday}) = 60\\% = 0.60\\).\n- The probability it will rain on Sunday, \\(P(\\text{Rain on Sunday}) = 25\\% = 0.25\\).\n\nSince the events are independent, the probability that it will rain on both days is:\n\\[P(\\text{Rain on both days}) = P(\\text{Rain on Saturday}) \\times P(\\text{Rain on Sunday}) = 0.60 \\times 0.25.\\]\n\nNow, we calculate the product:\n\\[0.60 \\times 0.25 = 0.15.\\]\n\nTo express this probability as a percent, we multiply by 100:\n\\[0.15 \\times 100 = 15\\%.\\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that it will rain on both days is \\(\\boxed{15}\\).",
