      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to use the concept of independent events in probability. Two events are independent if the occurrence of one does not affect the probability of the other. In this case, the probability of rain on Saturday is \\(60\\%\\), and the probability of rain on Sunday is \\(25\\%\\). Since these events are independent, the probability that both will rain on Saturday and Sunday is the product of their individual probabilities.\n\nThe probability that it will rain on Saturday is \\(P(S) = 0.60\\).\nThe probability that it will rain on Sunday is \\(P(U) = 0.25\\).\n\nThe probability that it will rain on both days is given by:\n\\[P(S \\cap U) = P(S) \\times P(U) = 0.60 \\times 0.25\\]\n\nNow, we calculate the product:\n\\[0.60 \\times 0.25 = 0.15\\]\n\nTo express this probability as a percent, we multiply by 100:\n\\[0.15 \\times 100 = 15\\%\\]\n\nThus, the probability that it will rain on both days is \\(\\boxed{15}\\).",
